00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.300
A couple of definitions.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:33.760
We'll talk about organizations,
folders, projects, labels,

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:34.750
and billing accounts.

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:36.530
And I'll dive into
each one of these

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:39.310
so you don't have to
speed read below here.

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:43.870
First, an organization
in the context of GCP

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:48.610
is this top-level parent of
all of your folders, projects,

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:49.840
and resources.

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:54.160
And it's a way to centrally
apply identity, permissions,

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:57.740
and policies across everything
in your organization,

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:01.750
in your own company,
or institution.

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:06.370
And organizations really give
you this level of comfort.

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:09.710
You can apply policy once, you
know it applies everywhere.

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:12.940
They're typically linked
to your company's domain.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:15.310
So you might have a G Suite
domain, whether or not

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:19.540
you use G Suite services, that's
tied to that organization.

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:22.240
And members of that
domain would have a set

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:25.490
of permissions within GCP.

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:28.660
Organizations contain
folders nested up

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:30.970
to four levels deep,
which are a way

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:35.620
to narrow down those permissions
and policies in a way that

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:38.620
mirrors the structure of
your company, of your teams.

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:41.110
We'll talk more about
that in a moment.

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:46.730
Once we get below orgs, folders,
we get down to projects.

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:49.490
These are the core building
blocks and the place

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:52.598
where workloads live in GCP.

00:06:52.598 --> 00:06:54.140
And they're the most
important for us

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:57.620
when we talk about how billing
is structured and reported

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:00.320
in our world.

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:02.690
Just about everything runs
in the context of a project,

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:04.800
and that's the most
important context

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:06.755
you'll see when you're
looking at your costs.

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:15.210
Now, a project can only
live in one folder.

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:18.170
So we have to have a way
to cross-cut and report

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:21.650
across folders, across
projects, anywhere you

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:24.750
want to see that labeled usage.

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:26.150
So our cross-cutting
structure is

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:31.040
called a label, which is
a key value pair, a label

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:34.220
key that you define, and a value
associated with it that you

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:36.740
can attach to a project
or the resources

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:38.270
below the project level.

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:40.160
We'll look at a bunch
of examples about that

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:41.118
in a couple of minutes.

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:45.710
Finally, got to a big
green dollar sign.

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:50.090
A billing account is
this container that

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:53.450
pays for usage within projects.

00:07:53.450 --> 00:07:56.060
And a project is always
paid for by a single billing

00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:57.305
account in GCP.

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:02.720
Once we have those
basic structures,

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:05.780
we can start to
use them to manage

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:08.450
the visibility of
our costs, seeing

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:11.000
what's happening at
each of those levels,

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:15.410
the accountability,
who's causing spend,

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:18.380
the control to apply
budgets and alerts

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:23.210
and to forecast that spending,
and intelligence to start

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:27.080
to reason about how to
reallocate spending,

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:29.060
how to drive it down,
and how to take control

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:32.580
of costs on our platform.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:36.150
So this leads us to our
first best practice,

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:39.650
which is the
finest-grained detail you

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:43.808
can get about billing is inside
your BigQuery billing export.

00:08:43.808 --> 00:08:45.350
And this is something
you can turn on

00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:47.420
for your entire GCP account.

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:49.800
It's worth turning
it on on day one.

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:51.740
It will give you the
most minute detail

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:54.710
we give you about everything
happening in billing.

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:57.110
And we'll look at example
queries and examples

00:08:57.110 --> 00:09:01.140
of that export in a
couple of minutes.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.080
All right, we're
into "Hello, World!"

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:05.428
This is where we write
a C program that's

00:09:05.428 --> 00:09:07.470
eight lines long that
prints a-- no, I'm kidding.

00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:10.910
This is where we talk
about the basics.

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:13.790
One project, one small company.

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:16.740
A startup with their dog.

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:19.400
So we have a billing
account and a single project

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:20.743
sitting under it.

00:09:20.743 --> 00:09:22.910
Within that project, we
have some workloads running.

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:24.440
And these might be
familiar to you.

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:27.860
There's many dozens of
services within GCP.

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:31.520
But in this project, we have
Compute Engine, Kubernetes,

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:35.810
Cloud Storage, BigQuery,
and Cloud SQL all running.

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:42.060
And they're all funded
by this one billing

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:44.310
account paying for the one
project they all reside in.

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:52.140
And I've drawn two different
color lines here for a reason.

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:55.480
The green dotted line
on top is pays for.

00:09:55.480 --> 00:10:00.870
The billing account pays for all
of the usage within a project.

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:06.180
Separately, the project owns the
resources contained within it.

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:09.300
I drew two color lines
because the permissions

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:12.750
on your billing account and
the permissions on your project

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:14.400
are separate.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:16.568
You can give a user in your
organization superpowers

00:10:16.568 --> 00:10:17.610
on their billing account.

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:20.010
They can be a billing
account administrator

00:10:20.010 --> 00:10:22.320
and have full view
of your costs,

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:25.320
but have no control, or
visibility, or access

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:28.260
to any of those resources
within a project.

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:29.650
It's a really important point.

00:10:29.650 --> 00:10:31.180
We'll come back
to it again later.

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:34.200
But that's why I have
this vision tests

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:37.470
to see dotted and solid lines.

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:40.080
We'll get rid of the detail
below just for simplicity,

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:44.130
and talk really about billing
accounts and projects.

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:46.740
So you were one developer
with your dog in a bag,

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:48.190
and you've now hired two more.

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:51.130
You hired Bob and Alice.

00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:52.138
Bob and Alice are great.

00:10:52.138 --> 00:10:53.430
You hired two great developers.

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:54.013
You got lucky.

00:10:54.013 --> 00:10:56.010
You found really good ones.

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:59.130
Now, Alice is a
really good coder.

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:03.030
Alice is not super up on
Linux systems administration.

00:11:03.030 --> 00:11:06.090
So she ran rm-rf thinking she
was in her home directory.

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:07.050
She wasn't.

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:08.400
She was at the root.

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:10.150
She's wondering why
that's taking so long.

00:11:10.150 --> 00:11:11.370
At the same time, Bob here--

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:14.100
Bob is JavaScript like
you can't believe.

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:17.140
But Bob and SQL don't
really get along very well.

00:11:17.140 --> 00:11:20.580
He wrote a update query
without a WHERE clause.

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:22.532
He's wondering why that's
taking a long time.

00:11:22.532 --> 00:11:24.990
You're wondering maybe about
whether you hired the greatest

00:11:24.990 --> 00:11:26.448
Bob and Alice you
could have hired,

00:11:26.448 --> 00:11:28.283
but they're going to be OK.

00:11:28.283 --> 00:11:29.700
Their faces turn
to frowns as they

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:33.250
realize they've done
something awful in production.

00:11:33.250 --> 00:11:35.250
And now we have our first split.

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:36.840
We need a second project.

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:38.430
We need a development
environment,

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:40.805
because Bob and Alice need to
get some guardrails on what

00:11:40.805 --> 00:11:42.000
they're doing.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:45.900
Both projects are funded by the
same single billing account,

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:49.050
but now we can divide
permissions between them.

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:52.140
We can look at the cost of
our production environment.

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:54.940
Might be many times the
size of our dev environment.

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:56.420
And we can put
them in some silos

00:11:56.420 --> 00:11:59.970
so they don't blow
away prod again.

00:11:59.970 --> 00:12:03.270
This leads us to
another best practice.

00:12:03.270 --> 00:12:06.990
Use projects to enforce
boundaries between workloads,

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:09.750
between teams, or
when you really

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:12.810
need to know about the cost
of one set of usage completely

00:12:12.810 --> 00:12:14.700
partitioned from another.

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:16.690
A great way to enforce policy.

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:19.650
Now, probably many of you
have multiple projects already

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:20.760
in GCP.

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:23.310
So let's move on.

00:12:23.310 --> 00:12:25.890
Let's look at how you actually
see the cost of these projects

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:27.265
and then add some
more complexity

00:12:27.265 --> 00:12:29.200
as we grow our company.

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:31.590
The first way you're
going to see these costs

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:34.440
is with your invoice at
the end of the month.

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:38.400
And your invoice tells you
your company, its address,

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:41.130
how much you owe
Google, and some degree

00:12:41.130 --> 00:12:45.690
of breakdown about those costs
on a project and resource

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:48.140
level.

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:50.250
The invoice is
great for one thing.

00:12:50.250 --> 00:12:52.320
It's great for telling
you how much you owe.

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:54.840
But it's not really
a great cost report.

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:56.610
You can't slice and dice it.

00:12:56.610 --> 00:13:00.300
There is both a PDF and a CSV,
and you can slice and dice

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:01.302
the CSV a little bit.

00:13:01.302 --> 00:13:02.760
But we'll look at
a much better way

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:04.500
to do those kinds
of questions, rather

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:11.640
than firing up a spreadsheet and
going and querying it that way.

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:16.140
Second tool, billing reports.

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:18.395
Now we're getting to
an interactive way

00:13:18.395 --> 00:13:19.770
to slice and dice
cost, and we'll

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:22.200
look at some real
examples sliced and diced

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:28.540
by project, by organization,
by label in a few minutes.

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:30.150
Finally, the BigQuery
billing export.

00:13:30.150 --> 00:13:32.400
I mentioned this at the
beginning, this first best

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:34.200
practice.

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:35.700
Really easy to turn
it on, and we'll

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:37.350
look at some real
data in a few minutes

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:39.660
about what you
can get out of it.

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:42.910
To turn on your BigQuery export,
go to your billing account,

00:13:42.910 --> 00:13:45.240
click on the
Billing Export link,

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:49.140
and you'll be able to
turn on that export

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:51.630
and put it into a project.

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:53.100
So another best practice.

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:55.620
Use the billing
reports and exports

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:57.570
to answer the why questions.

00:13:57.570 --> 00:13:59.970
Use your invoice to reason
about what you owe Google,

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:01.590
that kind of bottom line.

00:14:01.590 --> 00:14:06.900
By the way, if your invoice ever
grows beyond 25 pages in a PDF,

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:08.520
we'll start to
collapse it down so we

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:11.010
don't send you the world's
largest PDF to try and open

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:13.110
in your poor web browser.

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:17.910
The CSV version of the invoice
is full detail always, as big

00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:18.420
as it gets.

00:14:21.870 --> 00:14:25.140
So our startup is growing.

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:28.180
Bob and Alice now have
dozens of teammates,

00:14:28.180 --> 00:14:30.450
and we're going to
put an organization

00:14:30.450 --> 00:14:33.180
to help manage
policy and control

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:35.703
all of these projects
in our world now.

00:14:35.703 --> 00:14:37.620
Probably a best practice
to do from the start,

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:39.100
but the startup
didn't know that,

00:14:39.100 --> 00:14:41.940
so they're evolving as they go.

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:42.620
So we've grown.

00:14:42.620 --> 00:14:44.458
We have a whole
range of projects.

00:14:44.458 --> 00:14:46.500
We decided we wanted to
put a staging environment

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:48.540
in the middle of dev and prod.

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:50.790
We have our MacroService,
that old legacy big old

00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:53.940
pile of code somewhere
chugging away.

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:56.250
The founder made a
project called TopSecret.

00:14:56.250 --> 00:14:57.420
Don't look there.

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:01.110
There's an archive
for old stuff.

00:15:01.110 --> 00:15:04.380
But yay, we're growing
turns into, help,

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:06.270
we're growing when
you start to get

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:08.130
beyond the tens of projects.

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:09.630
Gets hard to reason
about the world.

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:15.190
Gets hard to partition policies,
permissions, and control.

00:15:15.190 --> 00:15:17.320
So what can we do here?

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:20.910
Well, first, we're working
on a feature which hasn't yet

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:23.580
launched, but will start
to roll out to customers

00:15:23.580 --> 00:15:27.180
later this year to have
one default billing account

00:15:27.180 --> 00:15:29.040
for the entire organization.

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:30.540
And when that's
live, you won't have

00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:33.900
to reason about billing
accounts on a per-project basis.

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:36.323
You can set it and forget it
at the organization level.

00:15:36.323 --> 00:15:37.740
So that's something
that's coming.

00:15:42.350 --> 00:15:44.810
Once we get into this
multi-project world,

00:15:44.810 --> 00:15:47.330
the same tools
still apply, but you

00:15:47.330 --> 00:15:50.660
start to have a need to
filter and slice and dice

00:15:50.660 --> 00:15:53.810
to understand costs below
the organization level using

00:15:53.810 --> 00:15:57.630
some of the structural concepts
we talked about earlier.

00:15:57.630 --> 00:16:01.370
So this is a real
billing report,

00:16:01.370 --> 00:16:06.763
and this shows a number of
projects all grouped together.

00:16:06.763 --> 00:16:08.180
There's a couple
of other sessions

00:16:08.180 --> 00:16:10.722
that go deep into this tool,
but I'll give you the highlights

00:16:10.722 --> 00:16:12.320
here today.

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:14.600
Billing reports let
you drill into cost

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:16.310
by the mechanisms
I just described.

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:18.350
And here we have them
broken by project.

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:20.780
So I've opened up this filter
to let me see by project.

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:24.830
I can take those off, and
change how I group and show

00:16:24.830 --> 00:16:28.040
cost over time to see what's
growing and what's shrinking.

00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:29.600
And obviously,
sometime mid-March, we

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:30.950
ramped up a lot of
usage, and we see

00:16:30.950 --> 00:16:32.242
that across all those projects.

00:16:36.050 --> 00:16:40.280
I can do a similar kind of
reporting using any of the data

00:16:40.280 --> 00:16:44.030
that we have in my
BigQuery billing export.

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:45.830
For those fans of
SQL among us, this

00:16:45.830 --> 00:16:53.090
is the 13 lines of SQL that
generated a report grouped

00:16:53.090 --> 00:16:56.540
by the project,
which is the project

00:16:56.540 --> 00:17:01.110
ID in this column under ID.

00:17:01.110 --> 00:17:02.970
The BigQuery billing
export is giving you

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:05.500
very fine-grained
detail on your usage.

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:09.329
So you'll have one row for
each couple of hours of usage

00:17:09.329 --> 00:17:14.990
by service, by application,
by project, by label.

00:17:14.990 --> 00:17:16.420
And you can query
it as you like.

00:17:16.420 --> 00:17:20.910
You can also press that magical
Explore in Data Studio link,

00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:24.000
and go out into a world of
visualization and reporting

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:26.940
tools that work incredibly
well with BigQuery.

00:17:26.940 --> 00:17:28.440
There are a couple
of other sessions

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:30.900
that go deep with actual
customer examples of that.

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:32.993
We'll talk about that
at the end and I'll

00:17:32.993 --> 00:17:34.410
let you know about
those sessions.

00:17:37.400 --> 00:17:39.948
The invoice also
ships a CSV, which I

00:17:39.948 --> 00:17:41.240
mentioned a couple minutes ago.

00:17:41.240 --> 00:17:43.620
For completeness, this
is what it looks like.

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:48.380
You'll see billing
account ID, project name,

00:17:48.380 --> 00:17:50.960
what product you're
running, the resource

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:56.180
type, networking versus compute,
IDs, dates, amounts of usage--

00:17:56.180 --> 00:17:58.220
the kind of fields you
would expect, but not

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:00.600
at the level of granularity
of our BigQuery export.

00:18:04.270 --> 00:18:05.770
So the takeaway
from this section

00:18:05.770 --> 00:18:07.540
from a control and
policy perspective,

00:18:07.540 --> 00:18:09.340
organizations are for everyone.

00:18:09.340 --> 00:18:11.920
They're a great thing to
turn on, whether or not

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:14.560
you use G Suite and
all of the other apps

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:16.510
that sit alongside
in the domain.

00:18:16.510 --> 00:18:19.540
Anyone can turn on an org
and its associated identity

00:18:19.540 --> 00:18:21.230
as a service for free.

00:18:21.230 --> 00:18:22.780
And that's a great
best practice.

00:18:26.230 --> 00:18:29.490
Now, we ended our
organization part

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:30.740
being a little bit of trouble.

00:18:30.740 --> 00:18:34.610
We had a lot of projects, and
the world was only growing.

00:18:34.610 --> 00:18:37.060
And you can start to
see the delineations

00:18:37.060 --> 00:18:40.100
where these projects
are really in buckets.

00:18:40.100 --> 00:18:43.360
First column is
stages or environments

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:45.890
of our application.

00:18:45.890 --> 00:18:47.530
The second column
is our MacroService,

00:18:47.530 --> 00:18:49.630
our old legacy stuff
that we broke out.

00:18:49.630 --> 00:18:52.008
We've got some acquisitions now.

00:18:52.008 --> 00:18:53.050
TopSecret is still there.

00:18:53.050 --> 00:18:54.080
Don't mention it.

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:56.380
Archive.

00:18:56.380 --> 00:18:58.600
We can put in a layer
of folders to start

00:18:58.600 --> 00:19:01.840
to control who can see
each of those resources,

00:19:01.840 --> 00:19:04.030
and start to manage the
cost and be able to report

00:19:04.030 --> 00:19:07.270
on cost by folder.

00:19:07.270 --> 00:19:09.910
And there are different
ways to structure it.

00:19:09.910 --> 00:19:13.390
In one form, you can
structure it by environment

00:19:13.390 --> 00:19:14.908
if that suits you.

00:19:14.908 --> 00:19:16.450
In another form,
you can structure it

00:19:16.450 --> 00:19:20.710
by the logical application.

00:19:20.710 --> 00:19:24.100
You could structure it by team,
by department, by country.

00:19:24.100 --> 00:19:26.710
You're going to align these
folders to how you work

00:19:26.710 --> 00:19:30.880
and the structure that makes
the most sense for you.

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:32.290
And you can have a lot of them.

00:19:32.290 --> 00:19:32.860
They nest.

00:19:32.860 --> 00:19:34.330
You can have as
many folders as you

00:19:34.330 --> 00:19:35.890
need to contain your projects.

00:19:35.890 --> 00:19:38.710
By the way, you can have
thousands of projects.

00:19:38.710 --> 00:19:39.580
No problem.

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:42.200
Platform scales up to
many, many, many thousands.

00:19:42.200 --> 00:19:43.740
We have customers with one.

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:47.960
We have customers with
tens of thousands.

00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:52.550
So folders mirror how you work,
but they're not the only way

00:19:52.550 --> 00:19:56.480
to structure resources, not
the only way to bucket them.

00:19:56.480 --> 00:19:59.510
And you can think of them a
little bit like a primary key.

00:19:59.510 --> 00:20:02.780
A project can only
be in one folder,

00:20:02.780 --> 00:20:05.420
but we need something
else to answer

00:20:05.420 --> 00:20:06.950
questions like,
how much should I

00:20:06.950 --> 00:20:10.970
spend on databases across this
entire set of applications

00:20:10.970 --> 00:20:12.470
and tools?

00:20:12.470 --> 00:20:16.333
Tough to answer by folder,
because I have databases kind

00:20:16.333 --> 00:20:17.250
of all over the place.

00:20:17.250 --> 00:20:22.340
Let's just take the app
folder, and expand that out,

00:20:22.340 --> 00:20:26.640
and see how many things are
actually a database here.

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:30.530
Well, I've got some MySQL
servers running on bare metal

00:20:30.530 --> 00:20:31.925
GCEs, so to speak.

00:20:31.925 --> 00:20:33.050
I've installed them myself.

00:20:33.050 --> 00:20:34.217
They don't run in Cloud SQL.

00:20:34.217 --> 00:20:35.450
Those are databases.

00:20:35.450 --> 00:20:40.500
So let's label those instances
with a key and value.

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:41.470
The key is type.

00:20:41.470 --> 00:20:42.820
Value is database.

00:20:42.820 --> 00:20:45.320
Now I can do some cross-cutting
reporting within this folder

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:47.480
and across these projects
to see how much I

00:20:47.480 --> 00:20:49.760
spent across my databases.

00:20:52.605 --> 00:20:54.730
Turns out I got databases
in a lot of other places.

00:20:54.730 --> 00:20:58.920
Let's say I add a bunch of
Postgres down in Cloud SQL.

00:20:58.920 --> 00:21:02.840
And I have backups in
production, really big ones.

00:21:02.840 --> 00:21:06.420
And I want to attribute
those costs to my databases

00:21:06.420 --> 00:21:07.670
that they're backups of.

00:21:07.670 --> 00:21:10.830
I don't want to confuse
them with other data that's

00:21:10.830 --> 00:21:12.760
running in my environment.

00:21:12.760 --> 00:21:15.630
So labels on Compute
Engine, labels in Cloud SQL,

00:21:15.630 --> 00:21:19.560
labels in Cloud Storage, and
now I can report across them

00:21:19.560 --> 00:21:20.835
to roll up cost.

00:21:23.970 --> 00:21:26.100
As I mentioned, there
are trade-offs here.

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:28.740
You can think of a folder a
little bit like a primary key.

00:21:28.740 --> 00:21:31.590
Each project can live
only in one folder.

00:21:31.590 --> 00:21:34.070
They can be nested, but it
can only have one parent.

00:21:34.070 --> 00:21:37.720
And you can think of labels
as a set of secondary indexes

00:21:37.720 --> 00:21:39.345
on your resources
and on your projects.

00:21:42.460 --> 00:21:45.398
It's worth being deliberate
in this design in both cases.

00:21:45.398 --> 00:21:47.440
It's worth being really
deliberate in your folder

00:21:47.440 --> 00:21:51.070
design, because while it's
easy to move projects around,

00:21:51.070 --> 00:21:53.560
it might be disruptive
for policy and permissions

00:21:53.560 --> 00:21:56.500
to be shuffling
folders all the time.

00:21:56.500 --> 00:22:00.220
In labels, it's worth
thinking about governance,

00:22:00.220 --> 00:22:05.000
about the keys that you require
and the values they contain

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:10.030
so that Alice doesn't say
type:DB on a label and Bob says

00:22:10.030 --> 00:22:12.610
type:database, and
all of a sudden,

00:22:12.610 --> 00:22:15.040
we're living in two
different reporting worlds.

00:22:15.040 --> 00:22:16.146
So be deliberate.

00:22:19.650 --> 00:22:21.750
Some workloads
will automatically

00:22:21.750 --> 00:22:24.810
annotate their resources
with labels for you.

00:22:24.810 --> 00:22:28.170
So if you use Deployment Manager
and certain other services

00:22:28.170 --> 00:22:30.150
will auto tag resources.

00:22:30.150 --> 00:22:32.910
And you can piggyback on those
to do reporting automatically,

00:22:32.910 --> 00:22:33.860
and cross-cut.

00:22:36.500 --> 00:22:39.410
So what does the
world look like if we

00:22:39.410 --> 00:22:43.550
want to cross-cut by label in
these two reporting contexts?

00:22:43.550 --> 00:22:49.640
First, in my reporting view,
I can both filter and group

00:22:49.640 --> 00:22:50.740
by label.

00:22:50.740 --> 00:22:53.280
And here I've done it
with my environment label.

00:22:53.280 --> 00:22:56.960
And I've got production,
test, and dev that cross-cut

00:22:56.960 --> 00:22:58.250
across all my projects.

00:22:58.250 --> 00:23:00.980
There are 18 projects that
are rolled up in this view.

00:23:00.980 --> 00:23:03.020
And this is the
usage of those labels

00:23:03.020 --> 00:23:04.895
spanning all of those projects.

00:23:08.370 --> 00:23:13.480
If I drop into BigQuery,
I can take just

00:23:13.480 --> 00:23:16.700
about the same 13 lines
of SQL we saw before,

00:23:16.700 --> 00:23:18.440
and I added a little bit more.

00:23:18.440 --> 00:23:20.260
So nested field.

00:23:20.260 --> 00:23:25.330
So we added a couple of lines
to both un-nest those labels

00:23:25.330 --> 00:23:30.040
and to put in a WHERE clause
where we have labels.key

00:23:30.040 --> 00:23:31.720
as env.

00:23:31.720 --> 00:23:34.720
Now we've cross-cut that
high resolution billing data

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:42.040
by label and we've got a
per-month, per-service cost

00:23:42.040 --> 00:23:43.660
for each of those
resources running

00:23:43.660 --> 00:23:45.830
across our entire environment.

00:23:45.830 --> 00:23:47.830
And of course, that magic
button is still there,

00:23:47.830 --> 00:23:50.440
Explore in Data Studio, if
you want to slice and dice

00:23:50.440 --> 00:23:51.520
and visualize.

00:23:54.730 --> 00:23:57.550
Labels are like
super-charging your reporting.

00:23:57.550 --> 00:23:59.890
The way we think about
them as a best practice is

00:23:59.890 --> 00:24:03.040
they shouldn't be the end
all, be all of your reporting.

00:24:03.040 --> 00:24:06.430
Don't rely
exclusively on labels.

00:24:06.430 --> 00:24:08.170
Start with an org.

00:24:08.170 --> 00:24:09.820
Put projects in folders.

00:24:09.820 --> 00:24:12.100
Use a sensible project layout.

00:24:12.100 --> 00:24:15.750
But then when you want to
supercharge, add on this layer,

00:24:15.750 --> 00:24:18.080
and you can really
dive deep into cost.

00:24:18.080 --> 00:24:21.020
So it's a fantastic way to help
with those gnarly cross-cutting

00:24:21.020 --> 00:24:21.520
cases.

00:24:24.860 --> 00:24:27.980
Couple of advanced topics, then
we'll leave a good bit of time

00:24:27.980 --> 00:24:29.680
for questions.

00:24:29.680 --> 00:24:34.400
First, as I mentioned at the
beginning, Billing IAM, Billing

00:24:34.400 --> 00:24:38.270
Identity and Access
Management, and Resource IAM,

00:24:38.270 --> 00:24:43.250
the roles and permissions on
billing accounts and projects

00:24:43.250 --> 00:24:45.290
are completely separate.

00:24:45.290 --> 00:24:47.660
You can give your
finance team billing

00:24:47.660 --> 00:24:48.980
account admin permissions.

00:24:48.980 --> 00:24:50.180
They'll be a super user.

00:24:50.180 --> 00:24:51.875
They can run their
queries in BigQuery.

00:24:51.875 --> 00:24:53.250
They can look at
billing reports.

00:24:53.250 --> 00:24:54.625
They can do
absolutely everything

00:24:54.625 --> 00:24:57.860
you can do on a billing
account, but they will not

00:24:57.860 --> 00:25:02.150
be able to go and actually
use resources, see resources,

00:25:02.150 --> 00:25:05.450
change settings in projects
because those worlds are split.

00:25:05.450 --> 00:25:08.993
That's a very
intentional choice.

00:25:08.993 --> 00:25:10.910
There are other roles
on billing accounts that

00:25:10.910 --> 00:25:12.890
offer fewer
permissions, the ability

00:25:12.890 --> 00:25:15.605
to create them and
view them, but not

00:25:15.605 --> 00:25:17.480
to have that level of
administrative control.

00:25:20.060 --> 00:25:21.798
There are a couple
of best practices

00:25:21.798 --> 00:25:23.840
once you start to think
about this kind of larger

00:25:23.840 --> 00:25:25.370
organization billing.

00:25:25.370 --> 00:25:28.250
One of them is parent
your billing accounts

00:25:28.250 --> 00:25:30.298
in your organization
just like projects.

00:25:30.298 --> 00:25:32.090
It's not required, but
it's a good practice

00:25:32.090 --> 00:25:34.280
to do so you get
that central control

00:25:34.280 --> 00:25:36.950
over the roles on you're
building accounts, too.

00:25:36.950 --> 00:25:39.060
Put all your projects
in your organization,

00:25:39.060 --> 00:25:41.990
especially if you're
in transition.

00:25:41.990 --> 00:25:47.675
And remove the billing account
creator role from your org.

00:25:47.675 --> 00:25:50.890
For the vast majority
of customers,

00:25:50.890 --> 00:25:53.330
one billing account is
the only billing account

00:25:53.330 --> 00:25:55.040
you'll ever need.

00:25:55.040 --> 00:25:57.470
A billing account corresponds
to a single invoice

00:25:57.470 --> 00:26:01.040
in a single currency, and most
customers are fine with one.

00:26:01.040 --> 00:26:04.370
Some very large customers or
customers with special needs

00:26:04.370 --> 00:26:06.200
will have multiple
billing accounts.

00:26:06.200 --> 00:26:08.180
They might operate in
multiple currencies.

00:26:08.180 --> 00:26:11.210
They might have a complex
jurisdictional situation

00:26:11.210 --> 00:26:14.020
or departments that really want
their own separate invoice.

00:26:14.020 --> 00:26:17.570
And in that case, they'll stray
and have multiple invoices,

00:26:17.570 --> 00:26:18.740
multiple billing accounts.

00:26:18.740 --> 00:26:21.680
But one is typically
good enough.

00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:24.980
Once you take that creator role
away, anyone creating a project

00:26:24.980 --> 00:26:27.200
will always land that
project in the org,

00:26:27.200 --> 00:26:30.740
and it will always be funded
by your org's single billing

00:26:30.740 --> 00:26:31.710
account.

00:26:31.710 --> 00:26:34.250
So from a governance
perspective, you're covered.

00:26:38.550 --> 00:26:40.260
As I mentioned,
fewer are better.

00:26:40.260 --> 00:26:41.700
And a couple of
advanced scenarios

00:26:41.700 --> 00:26:44.010
why you might stray into having
multiple billing accounts.

00:26:44.010 --> 00:26:45.843
And I'm happy to answer
questions about that

00:26:45.843 --> 00:26:48.630
if you have questions about
whether that's right for you

00:26:48.630 --> 00:26:51.510
and right for your company.

00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:53.760
There's a session which
I'll show on a slide

00:26:53.760 --> 00:26:57.060
later on the schedule,
CST200, which

00:26:57.060 --> 00:27:00.240
talks about a level above--

00:27:00.240 --> 00:27:03.060
in the Google
world-- above cloud,

00:27:03.060 --> 00:27:07.260
and how your organization
and its domain

00:27:07.260 --> 00:27:11.400
relate to some other things
outside the Google Cloud world.

00:27:11.400 --> 00:27:13.110
You might have an Ads account.

00:27:13.110 --> 00:27:14.850
You might be paying for G Suite.

00:27:14.850 --> 00:27:16.470
There's a separate
payments profile

00:27:16.470 --> 00:27:20.460
that lives above Cloud that
spans all of those products.

00:27:20.460 --> 00:27:23.250
And if you're an administrator
of an organization, especially

00:27:23.250 --> 00:27:27.750
a large organization, or have
a controllership or governance

00:27:27.750 --> 00:27:30.258
kind of concern, this is
probably an interesting session

00:27:30.258 --> 00:27:31.800
for you, and I'll
show you where that

00:27:31.800 --> 00:27:33.092
is in the schedule in a moment.

00:27:35.620 --> 00:27:37.190
Last best practice.

00:27:37.190 --> 00:27:38.680
Everyone in cloud,
every customer

00:27:38.680 --> 00:27:41.080
from the smallest
to the largest,

00:27:41.080 --> 00:27:43.780
get free Cloud Billing support.

00:27:43.780 --> 00:27:46.028
We have a wonderful
support team that is here

00:27:46.028 --> 00:27:47.320
to help with billing questions.

00:27:47.320 --> 00:27:50.420
We recognize that
billing can be stressful.

00:27:50.420 --> 00:27:51.820
And it's really,
really important

00:27:51.820 --> 00:27:54.280
that we answer your
questions, solve the concerns

00:27:54.280 --> 00:27:55.430
and problems you have.

00:27:55.430 --> 00:27:57.280
So please always feel
free to reach out.

00:27:57.280 --> 00:27:59.338
There are links throughout
the Cloud Console

00:27:59.338 --> 00:28:01.630
that lets you click through
and get a question answered

00:28:01.630 --> 00:28:03.820
by our dedicated billing team.

00:28:03.820 --> 00:28:07.150
You can grant that billing team
access to your BigQuery billing

00:28:07.150 --> 00:28:09.370
export, and they
will be very thankful

00:28:09.370 --> 00:28:11.830
if you do that if you have
a really gnarly question,

00:28:11.830 --> 00:28:14.020
because they'll
be able to go look

00:28:14.020 --> 00:28:16.540
at that same super high
granularity billing

00:28:16.540 --> 00:28:18.460
data that you see,
and help reason

00:28:18.460 --> 00:28:19.690
about questions and concerns.

00:28:22.420 --> 00:28:29.180
So in summary, enable BigQuery
billing export on day one.

00:28:29.180 --> 00:28:30.800
It's almost zero cost.

00:28:30.800 --> 00:28:33.200
We give you 10 gigs
of BigQuery for free.

00:28:33.200 --> 00:28:34.833
It's $0.02 a gig after that.

00:28:34.833 --> 00:28:37.250
You're going to be a pretty
large customer before you have

00:28:37.250 --> 00:28:39.980
gigs of billing export data.

00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:41.870
That'll be the last
cost you notice.

00:28:41.870 --> 00:28:44.570
So turn that on day one.

00:28:44.570 --> 00:28:49.780
Use projects to enforce the
boundaries between teams,

00:28:49.780 --> 00:28:53.330
or workloads, or
sensitive contexts

00:28:53.330 --> 00:28:55.370
within your applications.

00:28:55.370 --> 00:28:57.543
And those will be reflected
in your billing data

00:28:57.543 --> 00:28:58.460
at that project level.

00:29:01.090 --> 00:29:03.640
When you're trying
to answer why,

00:29:03.640 --> 00:29:07.640
go first to cost reporting tools
and second to your invoice.

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:09.070
Invoice is how much you owe.

00:29:09.070 --> 00:29:12.660
Cost reporting lets you do
that slicing and dicing.

00:29:12.660 --> 00:29:15.670
Organizations are for everyone.

00:29:15.670 --> 00:29:16.990
Identity as a service is free.

00:29:16.990 --> 00:29:19.750
We encourage you to use it,
associate it with your domain,

00:29:19.750 --> 00:29:22.600
and get that level of policy
and control of the top level

00:29:22.600 --> 00:29:25.150
from day one.

00:29:25.150 --> 00:29:27.280
Use folders to
mirror how you work.

00:29:27.280 --> 00:29:30.160
A project can only
live in one, so think

00:29:30.160 --> 00:29:32.750
about that as the primary key.

00:29:32.750 --> 00:29:34.510
And then supercharge
your reporting

00:29:34.510 --> 00:29:37.930
by adding labels that cross-cut
across resources and across

00:29:37.930 --> 00:29:41.650
projects to get that
extra level of detail.

00:29:41.650 --> 00:29:44.800
And finally, Cloud Billing
Support is here to help.

00:29:44.800 --> 00:29:45.592
Please reach out.

00:29:45.592 --> 00:29:47.050
They'd love to
field your questions

00:29:47.050 --> 00:29:48.342
and help with billing concerns.

00:29:50.900 --> 00:29:53.510
There are four other
sessions that I'll point out.

00:29:53.510 --> 00:29:56.060
There are 12 cost management
sessions happening at Next,

00:29:56.060 --> 00:29:58.060
but there are four that
I think are particularly

00:29:58.060 --> 00:30:02.570
interesting for this audience as
a deeper dive into these tools

00:30:02.570 --> 00:30:04.240
and into real customer data.

00:30:04.240 --> 00:30:06.853
CST202, which is
Better Insight--

00:30:06.853 --> 00:30:09.020
Measuring the Cost of an
Application on GCP [? as ?]

00:30:09.020 --> 00:30:12.560
[? target. ?] We'll be diving in
with some other billing product

00:30:12.560 --> 00:30:13.640
managers.

00:30:13.640 --> 00:30:15.680
That's today at 2:20.

00:30:15.680 --> 00:30:19.850
CST200 is going up
a level, and looking

00:30:19.850 --> 00:30:24.410
at how your organization
and your billing accounts

00:30:24.410 --> 00:30:26.300
relate in the bigger
picture, how to think

00:30:26.300 --> 00:30:29.750
about governance and control.

00:30:29.750 --> 00:30:33.980
CST201 on Wednesday afternoon is
a financial governance session,

00:30:33.980 --> 00:30:37.490
looking at budgets and alerts,
a feature we haven't really

00:30:37.490 --> 00:30:38.070
